1. The quantity underneath: the sun's height, Starbuck, 21 June 2026

   local time   sun's height
     12:00       +64.51 deg
     18:00       +25.85 deg
     20:00        +6.28 deg
     20:30        +1.83 deg
     21:00        -2.36 deg
     21:30        -6.23 deg
     22:00        -9.74 deg
     22:30       -12.82 deg
     23:00       -15.42 deg
     00:00       -18.97 deg
     01:00       -20.01 deg

   It falls smoothly through zero. Nothing in the column says where
   day stops; a yes/no answer needs a cutoff, and someone has to pick it.

2. Four cutoffs, four answers to 'when does night begin?'

   night begins at        cutoff    Starbuck    Times Sq
   sunset                 -0.833       20:49       20:30
   civil dusk             -6.000       21:28       21:04
   nautical dusk         -12.000       22:22       21:47
   astronomical dusk     -18.000       23:39       22:37

   In Starbuck the four answers span 170 minutes.
   Every one of them is exact to the minute and anyone can recompute it.
   They disagree because they answer four different questions.

3. Same sky, different verdicts: Starbuck at 22:00

   The sun is at -9.74 deg. Under each cutoff:
     sunset               night    (top edge of the sun leaves the horizon)
     civil dusk           night    (too dark to read outdoors)
     nautical dusk        not yet  (sea horizon no longer visible)
     astronomical dusk    not yet  (sky as dark as it gets)

   Two people who call 22:00 'night' and 'not night yet' agree about the sky.
   They disagree about the cutoff, and the argument ends once they say it.

4. No minute is the one: the sorites

   From sunset to astronomical dusk: 170 one-minute steps.
   The largest drop in one minute:  0.138 deg
   The smallest drop in one minute: 0.054 deg

   No single minute changes the sky by even a quarter of a degree,
   so 'if it is day now, it is day a minute later' feels safe. Applied
   170 times it carries you from sunset to full dark. The premise is
   false for every cutoff: at exactly one minute it fails, and the
   cutoff is what says which minute.

5. Where a cutoff is never reached: the lowest sun on the solstice

   The sun's declination today is +23.45 deg, so at local midnight
   it sits at  -(90 - latitude - declination)  degrees.

    latitude   lowest sun   last cutoff the sun sinks past
      40.76N      -25.79    astronomical dusk
      46.52N      -20.03    astronomical dusk
      48.00N      -18.55    astronomical dusk
      50.00N      -16.55    nautical dusk
      55.00N      -11.55    civil dusk
      60.00N       -6.55    civil dusk
      66.00N       -0.55    none: the sun never sets
      67.00N       +0.45    none: the sun never sets

   North of 48.55N there is no astronomical night in late June.
   Ask 'when does night begin?' there with the astronomers' cutoff and
   the honest answer is 'it doesn't', not a time.

6. A third way out: a degree of day instead of a yes or no

   Let  day(e) = 0 below -18 deg, 1 above -0.833 deg, a straight line
   between. Then 'day and not day' has the value min(day, 1 - day).

   local time   height      day   day and not-day
     20:30      +1.83     1.00        0.00
     21:00      -2.36     0.91        0.09
     21:15      -4.33     0.80        0.20
     21:30      -6.23     0.69        0.31
     22:00      -9.74     0.48        0.48
     22:30     -12.82     0.30        0.30
     23:00     -15.42     0.15        0.15

   At noon and midnight 'day and not day' is 0, as a two-valued logic
   insists. At dusk it rises to one half. Fuzzy logic keeps both halves
   of the dichotomy and reports how much of each; a cutoff throws that
   number away to get a yes or no.
